================================================================================
BUILT-IN TOOL RESULTS — WHAT THE MODEL RECEIVES NOW (ENVELOPE FORMAT)
================================================================================
Companion to builtin-tool-results.txt (the OLD, pre-envelope document). Keep
both side by side to compare: the old file shows the mixed TEXT bodies the
model used to receive; this file shows the ENVELOPE it receives today.

Source of truth: indirect-code-daemon (Go). Every example below is what the
model actually gets, generated from the code paths listed in the RASTREAMENTO
(tracing) section at the end. Error strings are verbatim.

Note on rendering in THIS file: the envelope-close tag is written as
`</result>` to avoid confusing the reader's tooling; in the real wire it is
`</tool_result>`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE ENVELOPE (read this first)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every builtin tool result is wrapped in one pseudo-XML tag:

    <tool_result type="ok" ATTR="value" ...>BODY</result>
    <tool_result type="error">SYSTEM ERROR MESSAGE</result>

- type="ok"    : a normal result (including a command that exited non-zero).
- type="error" : ONLY when the CALL failed — bad arguments, permission denied,
                 path not found, aborted turn, unknown tool, panic. Its body is
                 the system error message (the one exception to "body = content").

- BODY  = ONLY tool content: program output, file text, log lines, search rows,
          JSON data. No system prose, no "[Showing lines…]" notes, no "Command
          exited with code N", no status word, no file paths, and no separator
          labels (stdout and stderr are merged, exactly like a terminal).
- ATTRS = every system FACT, rendered in the order the tool declared them,
          after `type` (always first). Empty values are omitted. Attribute
          values are entity-escaped: & -> &amp;  " -> &quot;  < -> &lt;  > -> &gt;

WHY: anything the system wants to say must be a FACT on the tag, so the body
stays unambiguous. If a system note lived in the body ("(no output)",
"[stderr]", "Command exited with code 2", "No output yet."), a program that
printed that same literal string would be indistinguishable from the system.
Empty output is therefore `info="no output"` with an empty body, and a command
that really prints "(no output)" keeps it in the body — the two differ.

Escaping in the body: only the two sequences that could close or spoof the
envelope are escaped — `</tool_result` -> `&lt;/tool_result` and
`<tool_result` -> `&lt;tool_result`. Everything else in the body is byte-exact,
so program output that happens to contain XML is preserved.

Wrap point (single choke point): core.WrapToolResultContent, called from
core/agent.go runOneTool after DurationMs is stamped. Tools only fill
Content (pure body) + Attrs (facts). See packages/core/envelope.go.

